1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,500
A home is never lived in only once.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,000
People move out.
The coordinates stay.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Every 3D scan of a home captures
a single moment.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
We added the missing dimension: time.

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,500
Dérive Home is a spatial memory map.
One apartment. Three eras.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Change the year, and every object
returns to where it stood.

7
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:41,500
Think of it as version history
for a home.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The apartment is a textured 3D scan,
rendered in real time in the browser.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Orbit it from above, or step inside
at eye level and walk through it.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Each object carries a versioned record:
owner, location and story, per era.

11
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,000
Its movement across the decades
is drawn as a path through the rooms.

12
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,500
But a home's history is not finished.
Anyone can write the next layer.

13
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,500
Describe a memory in plain language,
typed or spoken, in any language.

14
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:32,000
A large language model decomposes it
into structured data.

15
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:38,000
Time. Object. Place.
People. Event. Emotion.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,500
The model is constrained
to what was actually said.

17
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Where something is missing, it marks
its uncertainty instead of inventing.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,500
Spatial grounding: "by the balcony door"
resolves to a real coordinate in the scan.

19
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Temporal grounding: the memory joins
the era of the home it belongs to.

20
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
From words alone, the system derives
a form: shape, colour and scale.

21
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,500
It also writes a counterfactual: the parallel
home where the moment went differently.

22
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,500
The memory materialises in the room.

23
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:30,500
Nothing is overwritten. Every version
of the home runs alongside the others.

24
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,500
A book. A camera. A lamp. A record. A mug.
A recipe notebook. An archive box. A plant.

25
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Four are generative 3D assets, created from
text with physically based materials.

26
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:52,000
Ordinary things, holding
what no floor plan records.

27
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Where this goes next.

28
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Scan your own home. Rebuild a remembered
object in 3D from a single photograph.

29
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Shared layers, so a family or a whole
building can write into the same space.

30
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,500
For real estate: a listing shows a space.
This shows a life.

31
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:21,500
For rentals: a handover
of what the walls already know.

32
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:27,000
For museums and historic houses: visitors
leave a memory, not just read one.

33
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,500
For families: a home preserved,
with its stories, before it is sold.

34
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Space becomes an archive that people
can read, walk through and add to.

35
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:46,500
A home is a sequence of lives
occupying the same coordinates.
